## Getting Used to TypeScript

So far, I think TypeScript is fine. I would not say that I have become proficient with it, but I also have not found it that difficult to learn. 
I already had some experience with JavaScript and Java, so TypeScript feels familiar enough that I am not completely lost.
At the same time, it is still something new to me, and I have definitely run into scenarios where I was not sure how to implement something.

One example of this was the Jamba Juice assignments. In the first assignment, I understood what the problem was asking me to do, but I struggled with creating the findMenuItems method.
In the second assignment, I understood the problem but had some difficulty figuring out how to implement the totalCost method.
The third assignment was more difficult for me because I understood what the program was asking me to do but I did not know how to put the solution together.
I spent a lot of time trying to figure out how the Inventory and Store classes should work.

I am aware that understanding a problem and knowing how to create the solution are two different skills. I can usually understand what the program is supposed to accomplish, but actually turning that idea into working code can be much harder.
I find it difficult and I can't quite wrap my head around how to tackle a solution even though I know what the end product must be.
I think some of that comes from still being inexperienced and out of practice with TypeScript and JavaScript

From a software engineering perspective, I do not know enough yet to say whether TypeScript is objectively a good or bad programming language. From my experience, though, I think it is usable and understandable.
I like that it is similar enough to JavaScript that syntax and ideas are familiar to me.

## Learning with WODs

I think the WODs are a good way to learn programming because they force you to actually write code instead of just reading about programming concepts, which can be tedious and you might lose interest.
I agree with the belief that one of the best ways to learn programming, like most things, is simply to do it.

However, I believe there is a difference between practicing under a time limit and demonstrating what you can accomplish when you have enough time. I think the WODs in class should be timed because the time pressure is part of the purpose of the exercise.
On the other hand, homework is something I personally do not think should be timed. With homework, I would rather have sufficient time to think through the problem, make mistakes, fix them, and then produce a solution I'm happy with.
I believe this is also an important skill in addition to working under pressure with time sensitivity.

The time limits can make this style of learning stressful. This feels especially stressful during the non-practice tests that contribute more to your grade and have a heavier weight of points.
It's stressful knowing that these tests contribtue a decent amount to my grade and only having around thirty minutes feels like a short amount of time for something that can be punishing if not done promptly.
Sometimes I may understand the problem but still struggle to write the solution quickly enough or have problems with my solution that I just can't solve with the time that we have.
It makes me feel like I did a bad job because my program works poorly or is incomplete.

I understand why the class uses this learning style. Software engineers sometimes need to solve problems quickly, and being able to think through a problem under pressure is useful.
However, I don't necessarily think that struggling with a new concept / programming language under a time limit means that someone does not understand or comprehend.
I think finding a balance would be nice.

## Experience with AI

One thing I have also thought about is the use of AI. We are allowed to use AI to our advantage in this class, and I have used Google Gemini once to help me with Jamba Juice 3.
I was struggling with how to implement the Inventory and Store classes, and I used Google Gemini to help simplify a solution I had come up with but was not satisfied with.

Yes, I could have simply chosen not to use it, and that was my decision, absolutely.
However, I think there is also a psychological aspect to allowing AI. If you are told that you are allowed to use something, it becomes much easier to rationalize and say, "Why shouldn't I use it?"
I know that I would probably learn more by solving the problem myself, knowing that AI is an available option, and one that is kind of encouraged, makes it tempting to use it when you get stuck.

While it did help me complete the assignment, I did not feel satisfied or that good about using it.
I felt like I was cheating myself of a learning opportunity; I would have learned more if I had figured out the solution myself.

Personally, I think AI should probably not be allowed for these assignments and the class in the first place. Take a hard stance that it's not allowed.
If a student writes something that does not work or they don't understand, I think it can be more valuable for that student to struggle with it and then fix the mistake themselves.
Making mistakes is part of learning programming.

Again, I understand that students are responsible for how they use AI, and I could have made the choice not to use it.
However, I think removing the option entirely and taking a hard stance against it would make it easier for students to learn.

## Conclusion

In conclusion, I think TypeScript and the learning style of this class are both useful for me, even if I find them challenging, stressful or maybe not even enjoyable sometimes.
I'm still learning TypeScript and recollecting what I had previously learned about HTML, CSS and JavaScript, and I am still struggling when I have to implement a solution that I conceptually understand but don't know how to code yet.
The WODs help with actually learning to implement instead of just learning concepts and not understanding them but I think the time pressure should be balanced with some work that doesn't have as much time sensitivity.
